# Basic usage
	> systemctl edit sshd     # (drop-in) important Drop-ins are for modifying existing services
Drop-in works like:
existing service + small override = final service
But NOT:
override alone = service
 
This opens an editor and creates:

/etc/systemd/system/sshd.service.d/override.conf

Anything you put there overrides or extends the original service.
You’re not editing the original file

The original unit (e.g. from a package) stays untouched.

That means:

Updates won’t overwrite your changes ✅
You can easily remove overrides later

Multiple drop-ins stack

You can have multiple files in:

/etc/systemd/system/sshd.service.d/

They are applied in order.

Useful flags
Edit full unit (not recommended For distro-provided services, unless needed)
systemctl edit --full sshd
Copies the entire unit file into /etc/...
You now fully control it (but lose upstream updates)

# Edit user service
systemctl --user edit myservice
Viewing what you changed
systemctl cat sshd

Removing overrides
systemctl revert sshd

Quick comparison
Command	Result
systemctl edit sshd	                    Creates drop-in override
systemctl edit --full sshd	            Full replacement in /etc
systemctl edit --force myapp	            Create  drop-in even if missing
systemctl edit --force --full myapp	    Create brand new full unit This is the correct command when you want to create a brand-new service from scratch.

How systemd loads a service

systemd builds the final unit from layers (priority order):

1) /etc/systemd/system/sshd.service        		← highest priority (full override)
2) /etc/systemd/system/sshd.service.d/*.conf 		← drop-ins
3) /lib/systemd/system/sshd.service       	        ← vendor (base file)

///////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\/////////////\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\

systemctl --user edit myservice

Without --user (system services)
/etc/systemd/system/

With --user
~/.config/systemd/user/

What systemctl --user edit does
Default behavior (drop-in)
systemctl --user edit myservice

Creates:

~/.config/systemd/user/myservice.service.d/override.conf

Same idea as system-level:

original unit stays unchanged
your override is layered on top

full override in user mode
systemctl --user edit --full myservice

Creates:

~/.config/systemd/user/myservice.service

Same logic as system-level --full.
